Here are implementations for case-insensitive partial matching across common database options:

## SQL Databases (PostgreSQL/SQLite/MySQL)

```sql
-- Basic LIKE query (works everywhere)
SELECT DISTINCT r.* FROM recipes r
JOIN recipe_ingredients ri ON r.id = ri.recipe_id
JOIN ingredients i ON ri.ingredient_id = i.id
WHERE LOWER(i.name) LIKE LOWER('%' || $1 || '%')

-- "chick" matches "chicken", "Chicken breast", "CHICKPEA"
```

## PostgreSQL (recommended for production)

```sql
-- Use ILIKE for built-in case-insensitive matching
WHERE i.name ILIKE '%' || $1 || '%'

-- Add index for performance
CREATE INDEX idx_ingredients_name_lower ON ingredients (LOWER(name));

-- Or use trigram index for fast partial matches
CREATE EXTENSION pg_trgm;
CREATE INDEX idx_ingredients_name_trgm ON ingredients USING GIN (name gin_trgm_ops);

-- Then query with similarity
WHERE i.name ILIKE '%' || $1 || '%'
-- or for fuzzy matching: WHERE i.name % $1
```

## Application Layer (TypeScript/JavaScript)

```typescript
interface Ingredient {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  quantity: number;
  unit: string;
}

interface Recipe {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  ingredients: Ingredient[];
}

function searchByIngredient(recipes: Recipe[], query: string): Recipe[] {
  const normalizedQuery = query.toLowerCase().trim();

  return recipes.filter(recipe =>
    recipe.ingredients.some(ingredient =>
      ingredient.name.toLowerCase().includes(normalizedQuery)
    )
  );
}

// Usage
searchByIngredient(recipes, "chick");  // finds "chicken", "chickpea"
searchByIngredient(recipes, "GARLIC"); // finds "garlic", "garlic powder"
```

## With Multiple Search Terms

```typescript
function searchByIngredients(
  recipes: Recipe[], 
  queries: string[], 
  matchAll = false
): Recipe[] {
  const normalized = queries.map(q => q.toLowerCase().trim());

  return recipes.filter(recipe => {
    const matcher = (query: string) =>
      recipe.ingredients.some(ing => 
        ing.name.toLowerCase().includes(query)
      );

    return matchAll 
      ? normalized.every(matcher)  // AND: all ingredients must match
      : normalized.some(matcher);  // OR: any ingredient matches
  });
}

// Find recipes with chicken AND garlic
searchByIngredients(recipes, ["chicken", "garlic"], true);

// Find recipes with chicken OR beef
searchByIngredients(recipes, ["chicken", "beef"], false);
```

## SQL for Multiple Ingredients

```sql
-- AND: recipes containing ALL specified ingredients
SELECT r.* FROM recipes r
WHERE (
  SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT i.id) 
  FROM recipe_ingredients ri
  JOIN ingredients i ON ri.ingredient_id = i.id
  WHERE ri.recipe_id = r.id
  AND (i.name ILIKE '%chicken%' OR i.name ILIKE '%garlic%')
) >= 2  -- must match both

-- OR: recipes containing ANY specified ingredient
SELECT DISTINCT r.* FROM recipes r
JOIN recipe_ingredients ri ON r.id = ri.recipe_id
JOIN ingredients i ON ri.ingredient_id = i.id
WHERE i.name ILIKE ANY(ARRAY['%chicken%', '%garlic%'])
```

---

**Key considerations:**

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| `LOWER() + LIKE` | Works everywhere | Slower, no index use |
| `ILIKE` (Postgres) | Clean syntax | Postgres-only |
| Trigram index | Fast partial match | Requires extension |
| App-layer filter | Simple, testable | Loads all data into memory |

For a small-to-medium recipe app, start with `ILIKE` or app-layer filtering. Add trigram indexes if search becomes slow.

