The `any` mode I showed already handles OR, but let me make the API clearer with explicit `and`/`or` naming:

## TypeScript Implementation

```typescript
type MatchMode = 'and' | 'or';

interface SearchOptions {
  queries: string[];
  mode: MatchMode;
  searchTitle?: boolean;
}

function searchRecipes(
  recipes: Recipe[], 
  options: SearchOptions
): MultiSearchResult[] {
  const { queries, mode, searchTitle = true } = options;
  const normalized = queries.map(q => q.toLowerCase().trim());

  return recipes
    .map(recipe => {
      const titleLower = recipe.title.toLowerCase();
      const ingredientNames = recipe.ingredients.map(i => i.name.toLowerCase());

      const matchedQueries = normalized.filter(query => {
        const inTitle = searchTitle && titleLower.includes(query);
        const inIngredients = ingredientNames.some(name => name.includes(query));
        return inTitle || inIngredients;
      });

      // AND = all must match, OR = at least one must match
      const passes = mode === 'and'
        ? matchedQueries.length === queries.length
        : matchedQueries.length > 0;

      if (passes) {
        return {
          recipe,
          matchedQueries,
          matchPercentage: (matchedQueries.length / queries.length) * 100
        };
      }
      return null;
    })
    .filter((r): r is MultiSearchResult => r !== null)
    .sort((a, b) => b.matchPercentage - a.matchPercentage);
}
```

## Usage

```typescript
// OR: chicken OR beef (either one)
searchRecipes(recipes, {
  queries: ['chicken', 'beef'],
  mode: 'or'
});

// AND: chicken AND garlic (must have both)
searchRecipes(recipes, {
  queries: ['chicken', 'garlic'],
  mode: 'and'
});
```

## API Examples

```
GET /api/recipes/search?q=chicken,beef&mode=or     # chicken OR beef
GET /api/recipes/search?q=chicken,garlic&mode=and  # chicken AND garlic
```

## SQL (PostgreSQL)

```sql
-- OR: any ingredient matches
SELECT DISTINCT r.*
FROM recipes r
JOIN recipe_ingredients ri ON r.id = ri.recipe_id
JOIN ingredients i ON ri.ingredient_id = i.id
WHERE i.name ILIKE ANY(ARRAY['%chicken%', '%beef%']);

-- AND: all ingredients must match
SELECT r.*
FROM recipes r
WHERE (
  SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT 
    CASE 
      WHEN i.name ILIKE '%chicken%' THEN 'chicken'
      WHEN i.name ILIKE '%beef%' THEN 'beef'
    END
  )
  FROM recipe_ingredients ri
  JOIN ingredients i ON ri.ingredient_id = i.id
  WHERE ri.recipe_id = r.id
) = 2;  -- must match both terms
```

---

**Quick reference:**

| Query | Mode | Matches |
|-------|------|---------|
| chicken, beef | `or` | Recipes with chicken OR beef |
| chicken, beef | `and` | Recipes with chicken AND beef |
| chicken, garlic, lemon | `or` | Any of the three (ranked by %) |
| chicken, garlic, lemon | `and` | Must have all three |

